  "summary": "Tinsel is a type of decorative material that mimics the effect of ice.",
  "description": "Tinsel is a type of decorative material that mimics the effect of ice. It consists of thin strips of sparkling material attached to a thread. When in long narrow strips not attached to thread, it is called \"lametta\", and emulates icicles. It was originally a metallic garland for Christmas decoration. The modern production of tinsel typically involves plastic, and is used particularly to decorate Christmas trees. It may be hung from ceilings or wrapped around statues and lampposts. Modern tinsel was invented in Nuremberg, Germany, in 1610, and was originally made of shredded silver.\n\nAccording to the Concise Oxford Dictionary, the word is from the Old French word estincele, meaning \"sparkle\".",
  "culturalContext": "In the 16th century the word \"tinsel\" was used for a variety of lightweight fabrics with a metallic effect made with silk and gold and silver thread, including \"tinselled satin\". The fabric was associated in England with Christmas festivities. A 1443 court case at Norwich heard how John Gladman rode through the town as the \"King of Christmas\" with his horse \"trapped with tyneseyle\".\n\nThese tinsel fabrics were used in elite clothes (regulated by sumptuary law), masque costume, and for heraldic banners and flags. Elizabeth of York bought \"tynsell satin\" in 1502 and the wardrobe of Margaret Tudor included \"tynsen\" in 1515. Tinsel and \"tilsent\" were a less costly alternative to cloth of gold. Tinsel was used for a veil or caul worn by Mary I of England at her coronation in 1553. Silver tinsel fabric, described in Italian as tocca d'argento, was used for the sails of mechanical ships at a masque at the wedding of Mary, Queen of Scots, and Francis, Dauphin of France in April 1558.",
